What is TCP?

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is a communication protocol that makes sure data reaches the destination correctly and in the right order.

If some data is lost during transmission, TCP sends it again.

Example

Imagine you're sending a 100-page PDF to your friend.

If page 50 goes missing, TCP will send only page 50 again instead of the entire file.

This makes TCP reliable.


What is UDP?

UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is a communication protocol that sends data without checking whether it reached the destination.

It focuses on speed, not reliability.

If some data is lost, UDP does not resend it.

Example

Imagine you're watching a live cricket match.

If one video frame is lost, you probably won't notice.

It is better to continue the live stream instead of stopping and resending the missing frame.

That's why streaming services often use UDP.

What is a Port?

A Port is a logical communication endpoint on a computer.

Think of your computer like a large office building.

IP Address = Building address
Port Number = Room number

The IP Address tells the data which computer to reach.

The Port Number tells the data which application should receive it.

Example

Your computer's IP:

192.168.1.10

Different applications use different ports:

192.168.1.10:80   → Web Server
192.168.1.10:22   → SSH
192.168.1.10:443  → HTTPS

Same computer, different services.

Port Number Ranges

| Range         | Purpose                   |
| ------------- | ------------------------- |
| 0 – 1023      | Well-Known Ports          |
| 1024 – 49151  | Registered Ports          |
| 49152 – 65535 | Dynamic / Ephemeral Ports |

